<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan Hus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1370–<a href="/years/1415">1415</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John Hus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan Hus was a Czech priest and reformer, rector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-university">University of Prague</a>, whose criticism of church abuses drew on the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a>. Summoned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> under a safe-conduct from King Sigismund, he refused to recant and was burned at the stake there in July 1415. His execution provoked the Hussite movement and wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and he was later seen as a forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-hus.jpg" alt="Jan Hus" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Murer 1587 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stimmer_Jan_Hus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
